// The OS-level "sleep this thread until the stop clears" primitive
// `worldResumeWhenClear` needs (#5750), and its wake.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS ALONGSIDE ./gcyield.bit. #5748 made that loop's tail call
// `worldYield()` (`sched_yield`) on every check instead of one in a thousand,
// which removed 22.1 G instructions from `bench/cases/allocpar` and moved its
// CYCLES NOT AT ALL (22468 M -> 22457 M, per-rep sign 9/21, identical to the
// null control's own 9/21). A yielding thread still owns its core: the cycles
// moved from userspace into the yield syscall rather than disappearing. What a
// mutator waiting out a whole mark and sweep needs is not a smaller share of
// the core, it is to leave the core — which is a BLOCKING wait on the word it
// is waiting for, woken by the store that clears it.
//
// TWO PRIMITIVES, NOT ONE, AND THE PAIRING IS THE WHOLE CONTRACT. `stopWait`
// sleeps only while the word at `p` still holds `expected`, atomically with
// respect to `stopWake` on the same address — the compare-and-sleep guarantee
// futex and `__ulock` both give, and the only thing that makes a wake
// impossible to lose (`./gcworldstop.bit`'s `worldWaitForStop` documents the
// Dekker half that closes the rest of the window). A `stopWait` that merely
// slept for a while, with the compare done by the caller, would reintroduce
// exactly the lost wake this pairing exists to rule out.
//
// SAME OS-SPECIFIC SPLIT ./gcyield.bit's header argues for, for the same
// reason: `syscall` (§11.8) is Linux-only, `extern fn` (§11.7) is Darwin's and
// Windows's only route to the kernel, and both reject even an uncalled
// declaration of the wrong one, so one cross-platform file cannot hold both
// bodies. The bodies live in `runtime/gc/{darwin,linux,windows}/gcstopwait.bit`;
// this file only declares the externs. `gc` is already a
// `PLATFORM_PAIRS`/`WINDOWS_PAIRS` member (`scripts/g2archive.sh`) and that
// listing is per-DIRECTORY, so a new file inside an existing per-OS `gc`
// directory needs no build-driver change.
//
// DARWIN IS THE ONLY PROVIDER THAT BLOCKS TODAY (#5750's own scope). The Linux
// and Windows providers keep the yield path verbatim — their `stopWait` is one
// `worldYield()` and their `stopWake` is a no-op — so this seam changes nothing
// at all on those targets until each is measured on its own hardware. A win on
// one arch is not a win until it is measured on both.

// Sleep the calling thread while the i64 word at `p` still holds `expected`,
// for at most `timeoutUs` microseconds (the timeout is a BOUND ON A MISSED
// WAKE, not the mechanism — see `worldWaitForStop`). The return value is
// deliberately unread: "woken", "the compare failed so no wait happened" and
// "the timeout expired" are indistinguishable on Darwin (measured, see
// `runtime/park/park.bit`'s `parkWaitReady`), and the caller re-checks its own
// predicate on every pass regardless, which is the only correct use of any
// compare-and-wait primitive.
extern fn bit_rt_port_gc_world_stop_wait(p: *i64, expected: int, timeoutUs: int): int

// Wake EVERY thread sleeping in `bit_rt_port_gc_world_stop_wait` on the word at
// `p`. All, not one: a stop releases every mutator that waited it out, and
// waking one would leave the rest asleep until their timeout. Returns how many
// were woken; 0 is normal and not an error, since the caller gates this on a
// waiter count that may have gone stale between the two.
extern fn bit_rt_port_gc_world_stop_wake(p: *i64): int
